# Struct-literal array fields built IN PLACE (docs/design/mutable-borrow.md,
# milestone M3.5). A struct literal used to lower every aggregate field into its
# own temporary and then copy it in, paying for the field twice; an all-literal
# array field is now written straight into the struct.
#
# The change is claimed to be UNOBSERVABLE, and this directory is the claim in
# runnable form: the four lines of expected.out cover a binding's initialiser, an
# assignment to an existing place, `redde` through the hidden result pointer, and
# a mixed literal whose path field must keep the old temporary, and a fifth line
# whose array element READS the storage the same literal WRITES -- the case the
# literal-only rule exists for, and the line that fails if it is widened. It is checked
# under the reference backend and all four differential C builds.
#
# What it does NOT check is the frame size, which is the point of the change and
# is measured by hand (`-fstack-usage`), per ADR 0015: a floor on a frame size
# would be a number with no consequence attached. Measured when this landed: a
# `S { n: mensura, v: acies<u64, 2048> }` literal went from two slots to one
# (32,808 -> 16,416 bytes), and `arbor_percurre` from 127,184 to 73,952 on
# Kiln's own mips64-elf-gcc at -mabi=o64.
TEST: expect-exit=0
